<%#
  T-37 / FR-73 — THE CHART FORM. *"a chart form that INSERTS the tag and stores no hidden
  state."*

  §9b.1 clause 4: *"`{% chart %}` has ~8 parameters. A small dialogue — type, source variable,
  x, y, series, drill on/off — that inserts the tag rather than storing hidden state. The
  author keeps a text template they can read, diff, export and paste into an issue; the
  beginner never types the syntax. Rejected: a GUI report builder storing structured JSON —
  it would fork the model, break `git`-style review of templates, and make FR-57's
  byte-identical export round trip meaningless."*

  So this writes ONE LINE OF LIQUID into the textarea and then has no further opinion. There
  is no hidden field, nothing is stored, and the template the author saves is the template
  they can read.

  --- IT IS HIDDEN UNTIL THE SCRIPT ARRIVES, WHICH IS THE JS-OFF ANSWER ---

  The editor is a plain `<textarea>` and works with JavaScript off BY CONSTRUCTION (there is
  no enhanced version to fall back from — `[OQ-M]` closed the CodeMirror path). This form
  cannot be: inserting text at a caret is what a script does. So the fieldset ships with
  `hidden` and `chart_form.js` removes it — meaning that with no JavaScript the feature is
  ABSENT rather than a button that does nothing. A dead control is worse than a missing one:
  it tells the author the plugin is broken rather than that this convenience is unavailable.

  The syntax it writes is documented in the README either way, so nothing here is the only
  route to a chart.

  --- WHY THERE IS NO "drill on/off" ---

  §9b.1's list includes it, and a `{% chart %}` tag has no such parameter: drill-through URLs
  come from the AGGREGATION — `{% sql_aggregate … drill: true %}` puts a URL on every bucket
  and the chart carries whatever it is handed. A control that wrote `drill: true` into a chart
  tag would emit a parameter the tag ignores, which is exactly the "stores state that is not
  real" failure this whole clause exists to avoid. The note below says where the switch is
  instead. Reported rather than quietly dropped (CLAUDE.md §11.3).
%>
<div class="box" id="reporter-chart-form" hidden>
  <h3><%= l(:label_reporter_chart_form) %></h3>
  <p><em class="info"><%= l(:text_reporter_chart_form_info) %></em></p>

  <%#
    NOT A `<form>`, and that is deliberate rather than an oversight: a nested form is invalid
    HTML, and a sibling one would be a second submit target on a page whose whole point is the
    one above. These are inputs the script reads; nothing here is ever posted, which is also
    why none of them has a `name`.
  %>
  <div class="tabular">
    <p>
      <label for="rrd-chart-id"><%= l(:label_reporter_chart_form_id) %></label>
      <input type="text" id="rrd-chart-id" value="chart1" size="20" maxlength="40">
      <em class="info"><%= l(:text_reporter_chart_form_id_info) %></em>
    </p>
    <p>
      <label for="rrd-chart-from"><%= l(:label_reporter_chart_form_from) %></label>
      <input type="text" id="rrd-chart-from" value="stats" size="20" maxlength="60">
      <em class="info"><%= l(:text_reporter_chart_form_from_info) %></em>
    </p>
    <p>
      <label for="rrd-chart-type"><%= l(:label_reporter_chart_form_type) %></label>
      <%# THE SIX FAMILIES FROM `ChartSpec::TYPES`, read from the constant rather than typed
          here: an unsupported type is a `Degradation(:chart_type_unsupported)` at render
          time, and a picker offering one would be a form that produces a degraded report. %>
      <select id="rrd-chart-type">
        <% RedmineReporterDashboards::Charts::ChartSpec::TYPES.each do |type| %>
          <option value="<%= type %>"><%= type %></option>
        <% end %>
      </select>
    </p>
    <p>
      <label for="rrd-chart-orientation"><%= l(:label_reporter_chart_form_orientation) %></label>
      <select id="rrd-chart-orientation">
        <% RedmineReporterDashboards::Charts::ChartSpec::ORIENTATIONS.each do |orientation| %>
          <option value="<%= orientation %>"><%= orientation %></option>
        <% end %>
      </select>
    </p>
    <p>
      <label for="rrd-chart-x"><%= l(:label_reporter_chart_form_x) %></label>
      <input type="text" id="rrd-chart-x" size="20" maxlength="60" placeholder="label">
      <em class="info"><%= l(:text_reporter_chart_form_x_info) %></em>
    </p>
    <p>
      <label for="rrd-chart-y"><%= l(:label_reporter_chart_form_y) %></label>
      <input type="text" id="rrd-chart-y" size="20" maxlength="120" placeholder="count">
      <em class="info"><%= l(:text_reporter_chart_form_y_info) %></em>
    </p>
    <p>
      <label for="rrd-chart-series"><%= l(:label_reporter_chart_form_series) %></label>
      <input type="text" id="rrd-chart-series" size="20" maxlength="60">
      <em class="info"><%= l(:text_reporter_chart_form_series_info) %></em>
    </p>
    <p>
      <label for="rrd-chart-title"><%= l(:label_reporter_chart_form_title) %></label>
      <input type="text" id="rrd-chart-title" size="40" maxlength="120">
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    <%# `type="button"`, so a press cannot submit the editor's form by accident — which is
        what a bare `<button>` inside a page carrying one does on every browser. %>
    <button type="button" id="rrd-chart-insert"><%= l(:button_reporter_chart_form_insert) %></button>
    <span id="rrd-chart-preview"><code></code></span>
  </p>

  <p><em class="info"><%= l(:text_reporter_chart_form_drill) %></em></p>
</div>
